## DESCRIPTION
## Determine the difference of the area of two squares from their
## perimeters. 
## ENDDESCRIPTION

## DBsubject(Geometry)
## DBchapter(Shapes)
## DBsection(Area)
## KEYWORDS('area','perimeter', 'square')
## Level(3)
## MO(1)

DOCUMENT();
loadMacros('PGstandard.pl', 'PGML.pl', 'PGcourse.pl');

# Select two random numbers to be the sides of the two squares.
($x1, $x2) = random_subset(2, 5 .. 10);
$area1 = $x1**2;
$area2 = $x2**2;

$ans = Compute(abs($area1 - $area2));

BEGIN_PGML

One square has a perimeter of [@ 4*$x1 @] inches.  
A second square has a perimeter of [@ 4*$x2 @] inches.

The positive difference of the areas of the two squares is 
[_]{$ans} square inches.

END_PGML

BEGIN_PGML_HINT
When you know the perimeter of a square it is easy to find the length of
 a side.  When you know the length of a side of a square it is easy to find
the area.
END_PGML_HINT

BEGIN_PGML_SOLUTION
The positive difference of two numbers is the result of subtracting the smaller
number from the larger number.   
The length of a side of a square is one quarter of the perimeter so the length
of a side of the first square is [` [$area1] \div 4 = [$x1]`] and therefore its area in [$area1] square inches. 
Similarly, the length of a side of the second square is [` [$area2] \div 4 = [$x2] `] inches and its area is [$area2] square inches.

So the answer is [` [@ $area1 > $area2 ? "$area1 - $area2" : "$area2 - $area1" @] = [$ans] `]
END_PGML_SOLUTION

ENDDOCUMENT();
